package scalar_facts

// Internal opt-in owned result for one source document holding a bounded set
// of ordinary pure functions over numbers and bool, which may recurse. A function's
// body is locals, assignments, if statements, switches over integers, while
// loops, loops over int ranges, breaks, continues and returns. Values and
// conditions are literals, variables, arithmetic, shifts, conversions,
// comparisons, logic and direct calls of functions of the set, whose
// operands are such expressions. This is not a general IR, and no
// compiler-private identity crosses this boundary.
//
// The compiler host passes arrays of flat records, so a module is one array
// of rows. Row 0 is its Source: `name` is the path and `text` the document.
// A rejected module has exactly one more row, its Rejection: `text` is the
// diagnostic's message and the range is its source. Nothing else survives
// a rejection, and it must never be emitted.
//
// An accepted module's row 1 is its Document: `name` is the shared source
// snapshot's document key. Then each function follows in declaration order;
// the first is the module's entry and takes no parameters and returns int:
// - a Function row: its `name`, result `type`, `count` parameters and
//   declaration range;
// - `count` Parameter rows: `name`, `type`, ordinal `value`, name range;
// - its body's statement rows in source order, each with its statement's
//   range. The body ends in a terminating statement: a return, an if
//   statement with an else whose two branches end in terminating statements,
//   a switch with a default arm whose arms all end in terminating
//   statements, or a while loop whose condition is the literal true and whose
//   own body has no break. Statements after a terminating one are still recorded,
//   though they never run.
// The statements:
// - a Local row declares variable `value` of `type`, its `name`, and its
//   `count` operand rows follow, its initial value. The variables are the
//   parameters, then the locals in declaration order;
// - an Assign row stores its `count` operands' value in variable `value` of
//   `type`. A compound assignment's operands read the variable first, then
//   the value, then combine them, since no operand can change a variable;
// - an If row is followed by its `count` condition operands, then the
//   statements of its consequence, then optionally an Else row, the range of
//   its alternative, and the alternative's statements, then an End row with
//   the If row's range. An else-if is an Else row whose one statement is an
//   If;
// - a While row is followed by its `count` condition operands, then the
//   statements of its body, then an End row with the While row's range. The
//   condition runs before each pass;
// - a For row declares variable `value`, an int named `name`, and its
//   `count` operands follow: its first bound's, then its last bound's, each
//   an int, run once before the loop. Its `literal` is the sum of
//   RangeExclusive for `..<` and RangeReverse for `for <`. The statements of
//   its body follow, then an End row with the For row's range. The variable
//   takes each value from the first bound up to the last, the last itself
//   only for `..`, or the same values from the last down, and the body cannot
//   assign it. A bound evaluated once cannot change, so neither end wraps;
// - a Switch row is followed by its `count` operands, its value, of its
//   integer `type`, run once. Then each arm in source order: a Case row with
//   the arm's range and the Switch row's `type`, its `count` operands, the
//   arm's value of that type, or none for the default arm, then the arm's
//   statements. An End row with the Switch row's range follows the last arm.
//   The value arms are tried in order, each value evaluated only when the
//   arms before it missed, and the first arm whose value equals the switch's
//   runs; the default arm, wherever it is, runs when none does. An arm does
//   not fall through into the next;
// - a Break or Continue row leaves, or ends the pass of, the innermost loop
//   it is in, through any switch around it. It has no operands;
// - a Return row is followed by its `count` operands, the returned value.
// A variable is visible from the end of its declaration to the end of the
// statements it is declared among; locals of a consequence, an alternative,
// an arm or a loop body are not visible after it, and a For row's variable is
// visible only in its body.
//
// Operand rows are in evaluation order, each after the operands it consumes:
// a Literal's `literal` is its value, or 0 and 1 for false and true, and a
// word's is its bits, as a float's and a double's are their IEEE encodings;
// a Read's `value` is its variable's ordinal; a Compare's `value` is its
// operator and it consumes the two most recent unconsumed operands, numbers
// of one type, or two bools for == and !=, left first, to give a bool; an
// Arithmetic's `value` is its operator and it consumes the two most recent
// unconsumed operands, both of its number `type`, left first, to give one of
// that type; a Unary's `value` is its operator and it consumes the most
// recent unconsumed operand of its `type`; a Convert's `value` is Clamp or
// Wrap and it consumes the most recent unconsumed operand, a number its
// `type` converts from, to give one of its `type`; a Logical's `value` is
// LogicalAnd or LogicalOr and it consumes the most recent unconsumed operand,
// its left bool, then its `count` right operand rows follow it, which run
// only when the left does not decide the value, and give a bool, its value;
// a Call's `value` is its callee's function ordinal, `name` its callee's name
// and `count` its arguments, the `count` most recent unconsumed operands in
// argument order. A condition's last operand is its bool; a return's last
// operand is the function's returned value, and a Local's or an Assign's is
// its variable's. A shift's count is converted to the shifted type before
// the shift consumes it, and an integer operand of floating arithmetic, a
// comparison or a float to double widening has its own Convert rows, as the
// ordinary compiler converts them.
//
// Ranges are half-open source-byte offsets into the source text; lines and
// columns are one-based source-byte positions. Fields a row does not use are
// zero or empty. The rows own their strings: a host snapshot of the array is
// the retained result, not a view into the producer's invocation.
pub Row :: struct {
    kind: int;
    type: int;
    value: int;
    count: int;
    literal: i64;
    name: string;
    text: string;
    start: int;
    end: int;
    line: int;
    column: int;
}

pub SourceRow: int : 0;
pub RejectionRow: int : 1;
pub DocumentRow: int : 2;
pub FunctionRow: int : 3;
pub ParameterRow: int : 4;
pub ReturnRow: int : 5;
pub LiteralRow: int : 6;
pub ReadRow: int : 7;
pub CallRow: int : 8;
pub IfRow: int : 9;
pub CompareRow: int : 10;
pub ArithmeticRow: int : 11;
pub UnaryRow: int : 12;
pub ConvertRow: int : 13;
pub LocalRow: int : 14;
pub AssignRow: int : 15;
pub ElseRow: int : 16;
pub EndRow: int : 17;
pub LogicalRow: int : 18;
pub WhileRow: int : 19;
pub ForRow: int : 20;
pub BreakRow: int : 21;
pub ContinueRow: int : 22;
pub SwitchRow: int : 23;
pub CaseRow: int : 24;

// The scalar types: int and bool, then the other integer types, then the
// floating ones. The facts carry no compiler storage ordinal.
pub IntType: int : 1;
pub BoolType: int : 2;
pub Int8Type: int : 3;
pub ByteType: int : 4;
pub Int16Type: int : 5;
pub Uint16Type: int : 6;
pub UintType: int : 7;
pub Int64Type: int : 8;
pub WordType: int : 9;
pub FloatType: int : 10;
pub DoubleType: int : 11;

// Compare operators: the source's <, <=, >, >=, == and !=, signed or
// unsigned as their operands' type is. On floats, each is false when an
// operand is NaN, except != which is true.
pub Less: int : 1;
pub LessEqual: int : 2;
pub Greater: int : 3;
pub GreaterEqual: int : 4;
pub Equal: int : 5;
pub NotEqual: int : 6;

// Arithmetic operators: the source's +, -, *, /, %, &, |, ^, << and >> on
// integers. None fails: they wrap, x / 0 is 0, x % 0 is x, MIN / -1 is MIN
// and MIN % -1 is 0, and a shift count at or past the width, read unsigned,
// gives 0, or the sign fill for a signed >>. Floats have +, -, * and /,
// IEEE at their own width, rounding to nearest.
pub Add: int : 1;
pub Subtract: int : 2;
pub Multiply: int : 3;
pub Divide: int : 4;
pub Remainder: int : 5;
pub And: int : 6;
pub Or: int : 7;
pub Xor: int : 8;
pub ShiftLeft: int : 9;
pub ShiftRight: int : 10;

// Unary operators: the source's - and ~ on integers, and ! on bool;
// negation wraps.
pub Negate: int : 1;
pub Complement: int : 2;
pub Not: int : 3;

// Logical operators: the source's && and ||.
pub LogicalAnd: int : 1;
pub LogicalOr: int : 2;

// A For row's range flags.
pub RangeExclusive: int : 1;
pub RangeReverse: int : 2;

// Conversions between integer types: `T(value)` clamps the value to T, and
// `T(wrap: value)` keeps its low bits. A floating value converts to an
// integer type in either mode by truncating toward zero, clamping to the
// type, with NaN as 0. An integer converts to double exactly or to nearest,
// a float to double exactly and a double to float to nearest. An integer
// becomes a float only through double, so it has two Convert rows.
pub Clamp: int : 0;
pub Wrap: int : 1;

// The bits of an integer type, 0 for bool or any other.
pub Bits :: (type: int) -> int {
    if (type == Int8Type || type == ByteType) {
        return 8;
    }
    if (type == Int16Type || type == Uint16Type) {
        return 16;
    }
    if (type == IntType || type == UintType) {
        return 32;
    }
    if (type == Int64Type || type == WordType) {
        return 64;
    }
    return 0;
}
// Whether an integer type is unsigned.
pub Unsigned :: (type: int) -> bool {
    return type == ByteType || type == Uint16Type || type == UintType || type == WordType;
}
// Whether a type is float or double.
pub Floating :: (type: int) -> bool {
    return type == FloatType || type == DoubleType;
}
// Whether `literal` is a value of `type`: 0 or 1 for bool, its range for an
// integer type, any bits for a word or double, and 32 bits for a float.
pub Fits :: (literal: i64, type: int) -> bool {
    if (type == FloatType) {
        return literal >= 0 && literal < i64(1) << u32(32);
    }
    bits := Bits(type);
    if (bits == 0) {
        return type == DoubleType || (type == BoolType && (literal == 0 || literal == 1));
    }
    if (bits == 64) {
        return true;
    }
    if (Unsigned(type)) {
        return literal >= 0 && literal < i64(1) << u32(bits);
    }
    half := i64(1) << u32(bits - 1);
    return literal >= 0 - half && literal < half;
}

// Bounds both sides check before reading counts: source bytes, functions per
// module, parameters, locals and statement rows per function, a switch arm's
// Case row counting as a statement, and operands per function, counted over
// all its statements together.
pub MaxSource: int : 16384;
pub MaxFunctions: int : 32;
pub MaxParameters: int : 8;
pub MaxLocals: int : 16;
pub MaxStatements: int : 32;
pub MaxOperands: int : 64;
